The feast is called of purifications, which no one would err in calling a feast, a sabbath of sabbaths, a holy of holies, having announced; for the whole mystery of the incarnation of Christ is summed up, the whole appearance of the only-begotten Son is described; in which the Christ child was carried and God was confessed, and in arms was brought as upon a throne the creator of our nature sitting, and a pair of turtledoves was offered to Simeon and Anna; to these, if it seems, the evangelical words are interwoven; for there we see the encampment of God; thus then Luke has set forth for us the history of the feast; when the days of their purification were fulfilled according to the law of Moses, they brought him up to Jerusalem to present to the Lord, as it is written in the law of the Lord, that every male opening the womb shall be called holy to the Lord, and to give a sacrifice, according to what was said in the law of the Lord, a pair of turtledoves or two young pigeons; he who served for us ought to offer purification for us, not sparing the pair of turtledoves, having not spared his own blood; behold, then, according to the law, above the law, as lawgiver he works, and, providing the freedom of the spirit, he does not reject the servitude of the letter; he does not set aside Moses, so that in due time he may become a trustworthy witness;

When their days of purification were fulfilled according to Moses' law, 'their' refers to whom; Joseph was neither father nor responsible for purification, nor did Mary or the child need purification; a woman who gives birth to a male is unclean for seven days; but Mary did not conceive by seed, therefore she did not need purification; if a woman gives birth to a female, she is unclean for twice seven days; the days are doubled because Eve gave birth to a weak vessel; but as you have heard the woman's transgressions, come and learn her achievement; a woman introduced virginity to life, carried God in her womb, gave birth to Him in flesh, and He renewed our race; she was the first to receive Jesus from the dead, the first to proclaim the resurrection, and the first to announce joy to the disciples; the scripture says 'a woman who conceives'; but the Theotokos was a woman who did not suffer the things of women; her womb did not receive the seed of a man, and the offering was not for her but for the whole race

For our sake Christ is circumcised, and baptized for us, and the purifications of the law are fulfilled on our behalf; if he weeps, he washes away nature; if he is struck, he frees our captivity; if he carries the cross, he lightens us of the yoke of sin; if he is spat upon, he delivers Adam from the spittle of the curse; when the days of their purification were fulfilled, they brought him to Jerusalem, as it is written in the law of the Lord, that every male opening the womb shall be called holy to the Lord; Christ, as we said, as a lawgiver fulfilled his own law beyond the law; for he did not open, but left the gate of the virgin closed, and did not break the seal
